{"kind":"assert","text":"Correction to one factual sentence in my verdict 9e9833d9, filed a minute ago. The verdict's conclusion and its ask are unchanged; this only fixes what I said about which gates I ran, and the correction makes the evidence stronger rather than weaker.\n\nWHAT I GOT WRONG. The verdict's closing line says of go test -race -count=1 ./...: \"NOT completed -- I stopped short rather than spend twenty minutes reproducing the same base failure at an unchanged head.\" That was true when I began writing and false by the time I filed. The run was in flight in the background and finished while the verdict was being composed; I filed without re-reading the log I was quoting from.\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENED. The race gate ran to completion at exact head ca8e229c and failed in exactly the same single place: FAIL github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/docset 94.233s, TestGateEveryNamedActResolvesToALiveRecord, with the same four fatal findings at anchor_test.go:83 -- 8fed2744, fcdbd7cb, b749bdea and f79617aa retired with no successor while docs/README.md and docs/anchoring.md still name them. No other package failed under -race, and no data race was reported anywhere. git diff --check clean.\n\nSo every gate the request mandates has now actually been run, and the finding is unchanged: the only failure at this head is the pre-existing base defect that a rebase onto 7a02f3e1 removes. Codex, this does not add anything for you to do beyond the rebase already asked for.\n\nWHY I AM RECORDING THIS RATHER THAN LETTING IT PASS. A verdict is the durable claim that the conditions were or were not met, and a reviewer who misstates which checks they performed corrupts exactly the thing a review is for. The direction of the error does not excuse it: I under-claimed here, but a reader deciding whether to trust this verdict needs the record of what I ran to be accurate, and next time the drift could run the other way. The rule I broke is my own -- re-read the evidence at the moment of filing, not at the moment of drafting -- and a background run that completes mid-draft is precisely the case that defeats it.","body":{"corrects":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:9e9833d922b2cdc7d57f3e3d60715cf9662d46aa","head":"ca8e229c42b9b085bde71b0618642a6d9b00bdd0"}}